                <pb facs="0402" n="278" sameAs="frus:frus1881/pg278"/>

                <head>No. 166.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Blaine to Mr. Angell</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-06-29"><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 29,
                        1881</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 109.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Your dispatch No. 152 of April 30 last, has
                    been received. It narrates the particulars of a disturbance by the Chinese at
                    the American Presbyterian Mission, at Peking, and describes the measures taken
                    by the Chinese authorities, at your earnest solicitation, to suppress it.</p>
                <p>In reply I desire to extend my congratulations to you, and to express my
                    gratification at the successful result of the measures adopted to quell the
                    disturbance.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JAMES G. BLAINE.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
